In confined geometries such as magnetic nanodots, the stability of
emergent topological textures is governed by a delicate energetic
competition described by the extended Heisenberg Hamiltonian, primarily
involving symmetric exchange, the Dzyaloshinskii--Moriya interaction (DMI),
magnetocrystalline anisotropy, and the Zeeman field. Accurately
inferring these atomistic parameters from two-dimensional magnetic
domain visualizations constitutes a crucial inverse problem, yet one
that is inherently sensitive to noise and small perturbations in the
data. Distinct combinations of atomistic parameters can produce highly
degenerate magnetic states with nearly indistinguishable free energies,
rendering the inverse reconstruction of Hamiltonian parameters
fundamentally non-unique and severely ill-posed. This structural
ambiguity limits the effectiveness of traditional empirical fitting
approaches and computationally intensive simulation-based searches,
both of which struggle to efficiently discriminate among competing
solutions. Although deep learning offers a promising route for rapid
parameter inference, conventional regression models often remain opaque
black-box predictors that compress the intrinsic multimodality of the
inverse landscape into oversmoothed point estimates with limited
physical traceability. To address these challenges, we propose an
inherently explainable, regression-based deep learning framework for
the robust inverse estimation of magnetic Hamiltonian parameters. Our
methodology is built upon a rigorous computational pipeline in which
Metropolis--Hastings Monte Carlo sampling is employed to generate
thermodynamically relaxed and highly accurate two-dimensional
magnetization fields. These physically grounded data are subsequently
integrated with advanced convolutional neural networks and vision
transformer architectures to perform inverse estimation of magnetic
Hamiltonian parameters. To enhance interpretability, we introduce
RAMs, a novel framework that extends
gradient-based attribution to continuous-valued physical targets. By
correlating localized spatial activations with the absolute prediction
errors associated with inferred physical constants, RAMs provide both
visual and quantitative insight into how the network's latent
representations relate to established magnetic phenomena. Extensive
benchmarking on a custom-generated dataset of $218{,}256$ simulated
configurations spanning diverse and complex Hamiltonian regimes
demonstrates that the proposed framework accurately predicts the
physically dominant parameters, the annealing temperature
$T^{(0)}$, the second-shell exchange constant $\tilde{J}_2$, and the
DMI strength $\tilde{K}_{DM}$, with $R^2$ scores reaching $0.91$, $0.93$, and $0.97$ respectively.
The Vision Transformer attains the highest variance-explained performance
across seven of the eight Hamiltonian parameters, while Xception
yields the lowest absolute prediction errors, with MAE values of
$0.039$~meV for $\tilde{J}_2$ and $\tilde{K}_{DM}$ in their original
physical units. An unsupervised UMAP-based clustering of the latent
space identifies four physically meaningful magnetic phase categories,
within which the predictive accuracy rises substantially in the
ordered low-temperature regimes, with the labyrinthine \& conical
phase reaching $R^2 = 0.978$ for $T^{(0)}$ and $R^2 = 0.970$ for
$\tilde{K}_{DM}$, and the helical phase reaching $R^2 = 0.968$ and
$R^2 = 0.866$ for the same parameters, while the disordered
high-temperature regimes concentrate the bulk of the residual error,
quantitatively confirming that the dominant source of uncertainty is
the partial observability of the Hamiltonian in the two-dimensional
$s_z$ projection rather than a deficiency of the models themselves.
The RAM-based interpretability analysis isolates the degenerate
magnetic textures, most notably chiral domain walls and skyrmion
cores, that govern the model's predictive behavior, revealing a
hierarchical depth-wise decoupling in the convolutional backbone and a
globally coherent, patch-level attribution in the transformer
backbone, thereby providing an architecture-agnostic validation of
the identifiability hierarchy. The proposed framework establishes a reliable, interpretable, and physically traceable strategy for investigating complex nanoscale magnetic systems.
